        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 890,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 691,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Computes the natural log of the standard normal cumulative distribution function for every element of a tensor.\n\nIt gives log-probabilities of far-out events directly - the chance of a reading 10 standard deviations below the mean is far too small for a float, but its log is a perfectly ordinary number.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nClose to 0 (just below it) on the right, where the probability is nearly 1, and curving down ever more steeply to the left, like a downward parabola: -0.69 at 0 (log of 0.5), -15.06 at -5, -804.6 at -40 - where ndtr itself has long since underflowed to exactly 0.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.logndtr\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number and never above 0.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-5 to 3 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is log of ndtr of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -5 to 3, drawn from -16 (bottom) to 1 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (start -40 still gives finite numbers).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.ndtr is the probability itself. t.special.ndtri goes from probability back to standard deviations.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 675,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Computes the natural log of the standard normal cumulative distribution function for every element of a tensor.\n\nIt gives log-probabilities of far-out events directly - the chance of a reading 10 standard deviations below the mean is far too small for a float, but its log is a perfectly ordinary number.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nClose to 0 (just below it) on the right, where the probability is nearly 1, and curving down ever more steeply to the left, like a downward parabola: -0.69 at 0 (log of 0.5), -15.06 at -5, -804.6 at -40 - where ndtr itself has long since underflowed to exactly 0.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.logndtr\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined for every real number and never above 0.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-5 to 3 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is log of ndtr of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -5 to 3, drawn from -16 (bottom) to 1 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch (start -40 still gives finite numbers).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.ndtr is the probability itself. t.special.ndtri goes from probability back to standard deviations."
        },
